Under s.10(1) of the Nigerian Copyright Act 2022, you have the exclusive right to do and authorise the doing of the following acts in relation to a literary work: 

(a) reproduce the work;  
(b) publish the work;  
(c) perform the work publicly;  
(d) broadcast the work;  
(e) make the work available to the public by wire or wireless means in such a way that members of the public are able to access the work from a place and at a time individually chosen by them;  
(f) make any adaptation of the work;  
(g) distribute to the public, for commercial purposes, copies of the work authorised by you to be produced; and  
(h) do in relation to an audiovisual work, any of the acts specified in relation to a literary work under subsection (1).  

Therefore, you get the exclusive rights listed in these sub-sections.

The Registrar of Copyright is appointed by the President in accordance with the provisions of section 83 of the Act. The powers conferred upon the office include:

(a) to keep records of all works registered under this Act;  
(b) to inspect any record or document relating to a work which has been registered under this Act;  
(c) to examine any person who is suspected of having an infringing copy of a work protected under this Act;  
(d) to seize and preserve any infringing copy of a work protected under this Act;  
(e) to institute criminal proceedings against a non-compliant person; and  
(f) to institute civil actions for redress of any infringement of copyright under this Act.  

These powers are outlined in s.91(1).

Copyright is conferred on a work if it meets the following conditions: 

(a) It is a literary, musical or artistic work;  
(b) It is first published in Nigeria; and  
(c) It is eligible for copyright under any international agreement or treaty to which Nigeria is a party or any bilateral agreement between Nigeria and another country.  

A work shall not be eligible for copyright if it is:  

(a) a broadcast or cable programme;  
(b) a sound recording;  
(c) an audiovisual work;  
(d) a photograph taken by a person other than the author;  
(e) a compilation of data or other material which consists mainly of information obtained from publicly available sources; or  
(f) a work consisting solely of standard mathematical or similar formulae.  

Therefore, based on these provisions, only certain types of works qualify for copyright protection in Nigeria.

As a Nollywood producer, you can stop others from doing the following acts in relation to your audiovisual work:

(a) importing for reasons other than private or domestic use an audiovisual work that is an infringing copy;  
(b) selling, offering for sale, distributing, letting for hire, displaying for sale or hire, broadcasting, communicating to the public, or making available to the public for commercial purposes, an infringing audiovisual work;  
(c) reproducing any part of the audiovisual work;  
(d) making any device, knowing it has the purpose or ability to be used to make an infringing copy of the audiovisual work; or  
(e) trafficking in devices or parts designed or adapted for the purpose of circumventing protection afforded by a technological protection measure that controls the protection of the audiovisual work.  

This is outlined in s.36(1).

The rights that the Nigerian station has in what you broadcast include:

(a) The exclusive right to fix and produce any audiovisual work or other object of cable television which is an adaptation of the work;  
(b) The exclusive right to broadcast the work;  
(c) The exclusive right to communicate the work to the public; and  
(d) The exclusive right to do in relation to the work, any of the acts mentioned in section 10 of this Act.

This is stated in s.17(1).
